WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:03.738
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.738 --> 00:00:08.599
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Some

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:13.370
people eat to live and
some people live to eat.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.720
I am definitely the later.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.637
In other words,
I have been a life long foodie.

00:00:19.637 --> 00:00:24.351
I'm sure most of you have fond
childhood memories, a first bicycle, or

00:00:24.351 --> 00:00:26.540
a lemonade stand.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.120
My fondest memory takes me back
to when I was three years old.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.586
Recovering from the flu, my mother had
forbidden me from eating mangoes, but

00:00:34.586 --> 00:00:36.693
my grandmother snuck me one anyway.

00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:40.726
And I remember ecstatically
running out of the kitchen,

00:00:40.726 --> 00:00:44.513
mango all over my face, and
straight into my mother.

00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:47.513
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I told her I hadn't eaten any mangoes,

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:50.740
of course, and
[LAUGH] even she couldn't help but smile.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.380
Reflecting on my childhood,
I am flooded with memories like this.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:01.180
Memories connecting food with a love so
ingrained in my upbringing

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.910
that I could not conceive of human
suffering stemming from hunger.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.410
But this changed when I visited
my father's ancestral village and

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.870
met the children of some of his
childhood friends, my peers,

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.580
enrolled in schools just for
the meal programs.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.830
In stark contrast, walking through
the dining commons at Penn State

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:29.100
barely a few days later, I saw trays
full of food being thrown away.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.240
And I remember feeling appalled at
the amount of food that was being wasted.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:39.140
That was the moment I realized
that our food systems are broken.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.170
But it's not just the disparity between
what we don't have and what we throw away.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.730
We see ample evidence of our
broken food systems right here

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:50.800
in our own supermarkets.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.750
I'm sure most of you
are used to seeing this.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:59.550
But coming from a culture where a milk
man brought us one type of milk along

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:04.272
with a cow to our house every morning,
this dairy fridge took me from odd

00:02:04.272 --> 00:02:07.754
confusion faster than
a Tesla can go from 0 to 60.

00:02:07.754 --> 00:02:09.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:02:09.155 --> 00:02:12.977
this fridge exists to cater to
a wider array of consumer preferences,

00:02:12.977 --> 00:02:16.950
because you and
I don't make food choices the same way.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.440
You could be thinking about health
outcomes and buying organic food.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.170
You could be thinking about environmental
sustainability and going local.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.506
You could be thinking about economic
fairness and going fair trade.

00:02:28.506 --> 00:02:31.993
And the folks at the back could be
thinking about intangibles like taste

00:02:31.993 --> 00:02:32.523
or color.

00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:35.423
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; I like to think of this as our

00:02:35.423 --> 00:02:37.010
food value hierarchy.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.450
But most of us share one fundamental
value in this hierarchy.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.120
I'm sure you all know what it is, price.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.710
Food retailers due as well.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:54.680
So low cost has been a predominant
driver in this industry for decades.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.220
But as some of the other values
have started rising in importance,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.580
food retailers are extremely
slow to catch up, why is that?

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.290
What is it that needs to be fixed?

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.240
So I asked the GSB community to tell me
what they thought needed to be fixed,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.130
what was broken with out food system and
how they would fix it.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.310
Wave a magic wand, as it were.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.250
And then I went and
spoke to thought leaders and

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.225
food advocates actually working
to reform the food system.

00:03:23.225 --> 00:03:28.370
80% of the GSB community that
I spoke to said that healthy

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.770
food was inaccessible, unaffordable or
environmentally unsustainable.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.450
The food advocates agreed.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.380
But I want to frame this
problem slightly differently.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.850
How is it possible that a vegetable
that is grown halfway across the world

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:51.460
can be produced, harvested,
packaged, marketed,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:56.350
transported, and distributed at
a price point that is cheaper

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.669
than a vegetable that is grown
sustainably just miles from here?

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.530
The answer is simpler than you think.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.120
The food we eat is so

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:11.690
cheap, because it has become a commodity
grown in a rigged economic system.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:16.220
A system that reallocates the cost
of negative impacts of production

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.680
from the cash register to our tax dollars.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.900
And the industrial complex that
churns out this food is big and

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.760
it's made it so easy for
us to prioritize cheap and

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.680
unhealthy food instead of demanding
healthy and sustainable food.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.470
In fact, the cards are so highly stacked
against resilient farming systems,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.530
that I fear that they will never
break through to become mainstream.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.080
Unless we change our buying behaviours.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.133
And I hear you thinking
this is great Judy, but

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:51.740
I'm one consumer against an entire system.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.810
Isn't that what legislation's for?

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.190
What can I possibly do?

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:57.970
I hear you but I disagree.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.560
Because it turns out you can do a lot.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:02.630
I have two suggestions.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.310
First, recognize the cost of
industrially produced food.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:12.880
A pound of conventional chicken costs
about $1.50 at the grocery store.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.710
A pound of premium chicken costs
closer to $2.70, much more expensive.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.840
But as consumers, we don't pay the full
price of that conventional chicken,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.340
because it's hidden in our tax bill.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.140
I've spent years thinking
about this problem.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.420
And after wrestling with data
from the UN Environment Program,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.580
the Sustainable Food Trust, the Food Tank,

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.810
you name it, I've come to realize that
when you add the cost of negative impact,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.300
so this could be the cost of
runoff cleanup at factory farms,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:46.750
incremental health care costs associated
with antibiotic resistance in the country.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.460
That pound of conventional
chicken gets closer to $2.60,

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.380
not that far off from premium chicken.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.010
In fact, on average when you add these
negative externalities, that's a 2X

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:03.650
multiplier on the sticker price of any
conventionally produced food product.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.080
We're all familiar with MPV,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.290
I like to think of this as the future
value of our present choices.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.412
And yes you don't pay
these hidden costs today.

00:06:13.412 --> 00:06:16.680
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; But imagine a future where you can't

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.690
get cardiac surgery because you
have bacteria that is resistant to

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.080
all the antibiotics in the market.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.050
Or a future where your child is
suffering from chronic asthma

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:31.030
because of environmental pollution and
imagine knowing then

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.450
that your food choices in the past
had something to do with it.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.806
So think about the next time you reach for

00:06:37.806 --> 00:06:40.640
that industrial chicken
off the grocery shelf.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.377
Well, maybe now you're thinking that
the price value that you placed so

00:06:44.377 --> 00:06:47.130
much emphasis on isn't quite right.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.190
So identify your other food values and

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.260
consider adding maybe just
one more to your hierarchy.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.083
I like to think of this
as value training wheels.

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:59.787
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This could mean different things for

00:06:59.787 --> 00:07:00.840
each of us.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.810
If you tend to buy prepackaged meals,
identify a store that you can trust to

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:10.740
curate a collection of these meals that
will embody your food values for you.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.760
I know I rarely look at product
labels when I shop at Trader Joe's or

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.230
Whole Foods, because I know they've
done some of the thinking for me.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:19.860
But I also love to cook.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.540
My roommate told me last week that
I tend to cook for at least for

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.610
ten people every week, even at the GSB.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:27.340
Some of you are sitting here.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:28.300
Thank you for coming.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:33.110
When I shop for groceries,
I tend to prioritize health items.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:36.961
So I decided to add my value training
wheels and went local last week.

00:07:36.961 --> 00:07:40.380
My grocery bill went up by $12.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.747
That is the price of three
cappuccino's at Cupa.

00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:49.280
80% of you said that you would be willing
to add that one value to your hierarchy.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.290
So why don't you go and try it for a week?

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.370
If you buy based on price now,
try to go local.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.690
If you already buy local,
try to go antibiotic-free.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.870
See how little your bill changes.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:05.230
Because the true drivers of
change in the food system

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.460
are those of us sitting
at the dining table.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.694
And when we as consumers buy products that
are aligned with sustainable food values,

00:08:13.694 --> 00:08:17.327
we create a demand that food
companies will respond to, and

00:08:17.327 --> 00:08:21.414
farmers will respond to the buying
power of these food companies.

00:08:21.414 --> 00:08:23.580
Everybody wins.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.940
Just think about the replacement
of styrofoam cups with paper cups,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.460
the abolition of DDT in the United States.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.980
Or even the more reason of massive
expansion of Uber into several cities

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.740
despite crippling political pressure.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.790
All of these changes
started with one voice,

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.340
the powerful voice of a consumer,
your voice.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:49.050
So, whether or not we acknowledge it, we
have the power to vote with our wallets,

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.730
so we might as well do it intentionally.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:57.510
And yes, these food choices are a
conscious investment in an alternative and

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:03.690
resilient farming system, but they're also
an investment in a better future for you.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:09.330
A future where access to healthy, fresh
and delicious food is a fundamental right

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:13.660
for everybody, and not just for those of
us who can afford to pay the premium.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:18.950
And because, at the heart of it all,
we are what we eat, and

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:22.660
what I am is a life-long foodie.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:26.040
And maybe you are too,
because you love food just like me.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.233
But as you leave this talk, I want to
implore you to become a farmie as well,

00:09:30.233 --> 00:09:34.175
so that you can change the system
more than the system has changed you.

00:09:34.175 --> 00:09:34.872
Thank you.

00:09:34.872 --> 00:09:38.378
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:38.378 --> 00:09:43.059
[MUSIC]

